A composable that owns its own mutable state internally (a text field that holds its own text) can't be controlled or observed from outside, and can't be reused in a context where a parent needs to know or drive its value. **State hoisting** is the fix: move the state up to the nearest common caller that needs to coordinate it, and pass the value down plus a callback to change it — the composable becomes **stateless**, taking `value` and `onValueChange` as parameters instead of managing its own state.

```kotlin
@Composable
fun StatelessField(value: String, onValueChange: (String) -> Unit) {
    TextField(value = value, onValueChange = onValueChange)
}
```

The caller now owns the actual `mutableStateOf` and decides what happens on change — validate it, transform it, sync it elsewhere — while the field itself has zero opinions about where its value comes from. This is the same principle as dependency injection applied to state specifically: push ownership up to whoever needs to coordinate it, and make the leaf component a pure function of its inputs, which is also what makes it trivially reusable and independently testable.
